<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Constantine VII</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/905">905</a>–959</span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus, Constantine Porphyrogennetos</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Constantine VII Porphyrogenitus was Byzantine emperor, nominally from 913 and in real power from 945 to 959. A scholar-emperor, he sponsored the treatise known as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/de-administrando-imperio">De Administrando Imperio</a>, written for his son, which describes the peoples around the empire, including the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/croats">Croats</a>, Hungarians, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pechenegs">Pechenegs</a> and Rus'. It is a main source for the early history of many Balkan and steppe peoples.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/constantine-vii.jpg" alt="Constantine VII" width="320" height="426" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Wooofer · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Constantine_VII_Porphyrogenitus_(2).jpeg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>For nearly a thousand years Hagia Sophia was the cathedral of Constantinople, the seat of its patriarch and a principal setting for imperial ceremonies such as coronations, which the emperor <b>Constantine VII</b> described in his Book of Ceremonies.</blockquote><a href="/work/hagia-sophia">Hagia Sophia · Reception</a></li><li><blockquote>The emperor (basileus) was held to be chosen by God and to be the living law (nomos empsychos), and his court, whose ceremonies <b>Constantine VII</b> had set down in the Book of Ceremonies, was meant to mirror the order of heaven.</blockquote><a href="/societies/byzantine-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Byzantine Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Romanos I Lekapenos, an admiral of humble origin and father-in-law of the young <b>Constantine VII</b>, was crowned co-emperor and governed in his place.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-17">17 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Byzantine emperor Romanos I Lekapenos, regent and senior co-ruler for <b>Constantine VII</b> since 920, was arrested by two of his sons and forced to abdicate.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/december-20">20 December in history</a></li><li><blockquote>At the urging of their sister, the Augusta Helena, a coup removed the co-emperors Stephen and Constantine Lekapenos from power and restored sole imperial authority to <b>Constantine VII</b>.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-27">27 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The young <b>Constantine VII</b>, son of Leo VI the Wise, became nominal ruler of the Byzantine Empire under a regency council appointed by his dying uncle Alexander and headed by Patriarch Nicholas Mystikos.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-6">6 June in history</a></li></ul></section>
